# THE PUBLISHED KERNEL IMAGE this checkout runs when it is run as a container (si#200), and the file
# si#201's launcher reads.
#
# WHY A FILE AND NOT A MANIFEST KEY. The launcher is bash. It has to know the image reference BEFORE any
# Python exists to parse simplon.yaml with - that is the whole premise of the second route - so the pin
# is a plain line a shell can read:
#
#     ref="$(grep -v '^[[:space:]]*#' deploy/image/image.pin | grep -v '^[[:space:]]*$' | head -n1)"
#
# WHAT THE LINE IS. One image reference, pinned to a version. Never ':latest': a launcher that pulled a
# moving tag would run a different kernel tomorrow against the same checkout, and `docker.pinned_image`
# refuses the shape for that reason everywhere else in this repository. tests/test_own_image.py holds
# both halves - that the line is pinned, and that its registry and repository are the ones simplon.yaml
# declares under `images: kernel:` - so the pin cannot drift from the image the release actually builds.
#
# WHEN IT MOVES. In the commit that publishes a new image. `release:image` is on demand rather than on
# every release (simplon.yaml says why), so publishing and pinning are already one person's single act.
#
# THE FIRST LINE EXISTS SINCE 2026-09-23, and it was written in the order si#313 decided: PUBLISH FIRST,
# PIN AFTERWARDS. `./simplon.sh release image` ran on the v0.22.0 checkout, `release:image` read the tag
# back out of the registry before it reported OK, and only then was this line written - so it names
# something that demonstrably exists rather than something somebody intends to push. Written the other
# way round, this file would name an image that is not there, and on a machine with no python3 the
# launcher would take the container route and fail where it would otherwise have said plainly that no
# image is pinned.
#
# WHAT THE PUSH REPORTED, kept here because it is the second source for the line below:
#   v0.22.0: digest sha256:f9e262fee0569b33855fd3fd32c63d86971e54f830dd2b32e57b887bc4f82c72
#
# THE FIRST ATTEMPT FAILED ON THE NETWORK and not on the credential - four layers were already up when
# one timed out awaiting response headers. The second run pushed the rest and read the tag back. Noted
# because the refusal text `release:image` prints on a failed push is about package scopes, which was
# the wrong diagnosis that time.
#
# WHAT IT USED TO SAY. Until si#313 the registry was Docker Hub, which needs a stored credential this
# repository deliberately does not keep, so there was no route to publish at all and this file carried
# a paragraph explaining the absence. ghcr.io needs none: `release:image` logs in with the token Actions
# issues per run, or `gh auth token` for an operator publishing by hand.
ghcr.io/marcozwyssig/simplon:v0.22.0
